{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794571184267,"sessionID":"ses_e2b4a243affexa25EPiSDt2Sxc","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4c72702002xEkJT8BTKfR6in","sessionID":"ses_e2b4a243affexa25EPiSDt2Sxc","messageID":"msg_1d4c704f6001rNY8Y6a5h7Ng5r","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ba673a6556c3ff3e","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && git diff 35c3188681..HEAD -- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md"},"output":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\nindex 883ac30d82..22b508c123 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\n@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@ trigger_phrases:\n   - \"native recommend\"\n   - \"skill recommendation tool\"\n   - \"compiledRoute enrichment\"\n-version: 0.9.1.0\n+version: 0.9.4.0\n ---\n \n # advisor_recommend Command\n@@ -28,9 +28,23 @@ The caller-supplied `workspaceRoot` is bounded by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.\n \n ### Compiled-Routing Enrichment (`compiledRoute`)\n \n-Each recommendation carries an optional `compiledRoute` field (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:268`, `z.record(z.string(), z.unknown()).optional()`). `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes()` attaches it only when at least one recommended `skillId` is both in the fixed 7-hub `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` set and currently permitted by the tri-state `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag (`compiledRoutingEnabledForHub()`, from `../lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts`). Otherwise the schema re-parse is skipped entirely and the output returns unchanged. When enrichment runs, `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub <skillId> --prompt-stdin`, writes the prompt to its stdin and attaches the parsed result. The one exception is a result whose `servingAuthority` is `\"legacy\"`: the field then stays absent, because a legacy sentinel is never surfaced as if it were a compiled decision. A subprocess failure is handled the same way: silently no `compiledRoute` field, with an optional debug-gated stderr breadcrumb via `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING_DEBUG`, so a probe failure degrades to exactly what an ineligible or flag-off recommendation already looks like. In short, `compiledRoute` is attached only for an eligible, flag-permitted hub that is genuinely serving compiled. It is intentionally absent for every other combination: flag off, hub not in `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS`, legacy sentinel or probe failure. See [`feature-flag-governance.md`](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) for the flag's phased-default and serving-status contract, and each eligible hub's own `compiled-routing-and-legacy-fallback.md` catalog leaf for the hub-side directive this enrichment mirrors. The front door runs from the request's `workspaceRoot`, and a caller may name another checkout there, such as a worktree under the repository root. So the handler first checks that the front door it would run reads `--prompt-stdin`. An older front door gets no call and the recommendation carries no `compiledRoute`, because it would route an empty prompt.\n+Each recommendation carries an optional `compiledRoute` field (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:308`, `z.record(z.string(), z.unknown()).optional()`). `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes()` attaches it only when at least one recommended `skillId` is both in the fixed 7-hub `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` set and currently permitted by the tri-state `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag (`compiledRoutingEnabledForHub()`, from `../lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts`). Otherwise the schema re-parse is skipped entirely and the output returns unchanged. When enrichment runs, `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub <skillId> --prompt-stdin`, writes the prompt to its stdin and attaches the parsed result. The one exception is a result whose `servingAuthority` is `\"legacy\"`: the field then stays absent, because a legacy sentinel is never surfaced as if it were a compiled decision. A subprocess failure is handled the same way: silently no `compiledRoute` field, with an optional debug-gated stderr breadcrumb via `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING_DEBUG`, so a probe failure degrades to exactly what an ineligible or flag-off recommendation already looks like. In short, `compiledRoute` is attached only for an eligible, flag-permitted hub that is genuinely serving compiled. It is intentionally absent for every other combination: flag off, hub not in `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS`, legacy sentinel or probe failure. See [`feature-flag-governance.md`](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) for the flag's phased-default and serving-status contract, and each eligible hub's own `compiled-routing-and-legacy-fallback.md` catalog leaf for the hub-side directive this enrichment mirrors. The front door runs from the request's `workspaceRoot`, and a caller may name another checkout there, such as a worktree under the repository root. So the handler first checks that the front door it would run reads `--prompt-stdin`. An older front door gets no call and the recommendation carries no `compiledRoute`, because it would route an empty prompt.\n \n-The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:500-510`, `570-576`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute`. The prompt hooks always pass `false` because they never read the field (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:230`). The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:227`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:40`).\n+When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:310`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must type the same command or hold a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command. A prompt that matches `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` gets no command from any hub, because a reader who rules out a change is never handed work that writes.\n+\n+The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:563-565`, `632-634`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:287`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n+\n+The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute` or `command`. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:290`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n+\n+A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\n+\n+### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n+\n+Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n+\n+`lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, mutating?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `mutating` is the class the renderer's child downgrade reads: `/doctor:env list` carries `read-only` and stays fully visible to a dispatched child, while `start`, `ask` and a writing `run` are marked as parent-named. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy`, the table keyed by runnable string that covers every tracked command, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n+\n+The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n \n ---\n \n@@ -43,6 +57,7 @@ The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that de\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` | Handler | Source reference |\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` | Schema | Source reference |\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tools/` | Command surface | Source reference |\n+| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Shared | `attachCommand()`, the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table and the `DEPTH_WORDING` gate that name the slash command for a single routed mode. |\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts` | Shared | `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` eligibility set, `DEFAULT_ON_HUBS` cohort, and the tri-state flag parser the enrichment gate reads. |\n | `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs` | Script | Promoted CLI front door `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to. |\n \n@@ -51,7 +66,7 @@ The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that de\n | File | Type | Role |\n |---|---|---|\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n-| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. |\n+| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. `compiledRouteTopOnly: true` looks up the first recommendation alone. A command that ranks through its own row is not offered again by its hub. |\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-privacy.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/compiled-routing-consumption.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Bridge child-env allowlist forwarding, `buildNativeBrief` decision threading, and `=0` cache-invalidation coverage for the `compiledRoute` enrichment. |\n | `.skilled/bin/compiled-routing-flag-propagation.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Tri-state flag propagation coverage. |\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md\nindex 20f4fb025f..467e47a634 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md\n@@ -6,7 +6,7 @@ trigger_phrases:\n   - \"claude user-prompt-submit\"\n   - \"hookSpecificOutput claude\"\n   - \"claude advisor hook\"\n-version: 0.11.3.0\n+version: 0.11.5.0\n ---\n \n # Claude Code user-prompt-submit Hook\n@@ -23,7 +23,7 @@ Surface skill recommendations in Claude Code sessions at prompt time, without bl\n \n `hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` reads the prompt from stdin, calls the native advisor and returns a JSON envelope with `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. The hook honors `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` and fails open on any daemon-level failure. As a default-off, fail-open shadow it also records post-emission advisor-policy delivery (`observeEmittedAdvisorPolicy`), an observed receipt with `lifecycleEpoch >= 1`, as the final step before returning, leaving the emitted envelope byte-identical. Freshness vocabulary is `live / stale / absent / unavailable`. Status vocabulary is `ok / skipped / degraded / fail_open`.\n \n-Before any CLI spawn the hook runs the casual-prompt gate. `/help`, short acknowledgements and prompts below the length threshold return `skipped` without spawning the CLI. The CLI request sends `includeCompiledRoute: false`, so no compiled-route child process starts.\n+Before any CLI spawn the hook runs the casual-prompt gate. `/help`, short acknowledgements and prompts below the length threshold return `skipped` without spawning the CLI. The CLI request sends `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. It also sends `compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, so only the top pick gets a route lookup and only the top pick can carry a command. The hook keeps the `command` field on each recommendation and reads nothing from the route itself.\n \n ### Fallback And Repeats\n \n@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@ Directive-lifecycle dedup treats that fallback like a brief. In a known, confirm\n \n Hook diagnostics are prompt-safe rollups handled by `lib/metrics.ts`. The `spec_kit.<group>.<metric_name>` instrumentation namespace emits only when `SPECKIT_METRICS_ENABLED=true`. Default off is no emission and is byte-identical to the pre-instrumentation surface. Alert thresholds for the rolling cache-hit p95 are read from the environment with conservative defaults: `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_HOOK_CACHE_HIT_P95_WARN_MS` (default 75) and `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_HOOK_CACHE_HIT_P95_PAGE_MS` (default 150). Both flags are forwarded to the daemon child through the launcher `CHILD_ENV_ALLOWLIST` so the same thresholds reach every runtime.\n \n-Each record carries `runtime`, taken from `SPECKIT_RUNTIME` when it names a known runtime and `claude` otherwise. Records carry optional `emittedBytes` and `directivesSuppressed`. The hook writes records only when `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` is set. Raw prompts never appear in diagnostics. Before exiting, the hook waits up to 100 ms for pending diagnostic writes.\n+Each record carries `runtime`, taken from `SPECKIT_RUNTIME` when it names a known runtime and `claude` otherwise. Records carry optional `emittedBytes` and `directivesSuppressed`. The hook writes a health record to the diagnostics sink on every prompt and adds the skill label and the error text there only when `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` is set. A record whose answer came from the CLI's local scorer carries `degraded: true`. Raw prompts never appear in diagnostics. Before exiting, the hook waits up to 100 ms for pending diagnostic writes.\n \n ---\n \ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\nindex bddf8cc99a..a6b24e56bb 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\n@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@ trigger_phrases:\n   - \"advisor hook smoke tests\"\n importance_tier: important\n contextType: implementation\n-version: 3.6.5.0\n+version: 3.6.9.0\n ---\n \n # Skill Advisor Hook Reference\n@@ -35,12 +35,47 @@ On each user-prompt event the adapter:\n 1. **Kill-switch.** Checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` (master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED` or the `skill-advisor` family). Disabled → empty/skipped prompt-safe output.\n 2. **Read the prompt + workspace.** Resolves the workspace root (install-anchored walk, not CWD-relative, so the hook stays correct off-root), reads the prompt text (bounded), and resolves the session id.\n 3. **Prompt gate.** `skippedAdvisorResultFor` uses `shouldFireAdvisor` to decline skip commands, short acknowledgements and prompts below its length threshold. A declined prompt returns a skipped result and does not spawn the CLI.\n-4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: false`, so the daemon skips compiled-route enrichment and starts no `compiled-route.cjs` child. A daemon still running an older build rejects that option with JSON-RPC `-32602` and the CLI retries once without it.\n-5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n+4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. It also carries `compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, because each route lookup is a subprocess inside the hook's budget and the brief leads with the top pick. The hook keeps the `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. A daemon still running an older build rejects these options with JSON-RPC `-32602`, and the CLI retries once without them.\n+5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. With `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`, a surfaced `start` or `ask` command, or a `run` command that is not read-only, renders as `(parent-named only)`. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n 6. **Directive-lifecycle dedup.** `decideDirectiveLifecycleDelivery` suppresses a re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence, so a repeated prompt does not re-inject an identical brief. A repeat in a known session keeps the first line, a route or a fallback status, and drops the directives block.\n 7. **Emit.** Native adapters emit a `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON envelope. OpenCode appends to `output.system` via `experimental.chat.system.transform`. Pi sends a model-visible message.\n \n-The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the recommended skill(s) with their confidence. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n+The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n+\n+### Brief head form\n+\n+A recommendation that resolves to a command renders one label, shared by the `use` and `ambiguous` heads:\n+\n+```text\n+<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\n+```\n+\n+The recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A recommendation with no command row keeps its skill label rather than a command label.\n+\n+The `use` head reads `Advisor: <freshness>; use <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` For example:\n+\n+```text\n+Advisor: live; use system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start) 0.89/0.12 pass.\n+```\n+\n+The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\n+\n+A hub whose compiled route defers or asks to clarify, and whose modes have commands, renders as `<hub> (needs a mode)` with no command, for example:\n+\n+```text\n+Advisor: live; use system-deep-loop (needs a mode) 0.82/0.20 pass.\n+```\n+\n+The recommendation carries `needsMode: true`. A command on the same recommendation wins and the label is dropped.\n+\n+### Degraded contract\n+\n+When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\n+\n+- A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n+- Every other row collapses to its owner skill: `skillId` is `ownerSkillId` and `kind` is `skill`. It is dropped when that skill is already offered, so the degraded brief never repeats a skill.\n+\n+Rows below the threshold are discarded before either rule applies. The degraded brief therefore keeps the same head form and names a skill that exists rather than a raw row id.\n \n ---\n \n@@ -56,9 +91,24 @@ Every native runtime and Pi run the same hook handler, which gates the prompt, c\n | **Devin** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.devin/hooks.v1.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 10s | Same shim dispatch. `cd \"${DEVIN_PROJECT_DIR:-$PWD}\"`. Fallback `printf` envelope | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n | **Pi** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` (real file, re-exported by its one-line `.pi/extensions/` entry) | `user_input` / prompt event via `.pi/extensions/` | Imports the advisor module **in-process** (not a subprocess). Pi awaits input handlers before agent processing, so the old blocking-spawn bridge stalled every send. In-process calls remove the stall and let the module-level prompt cache work. Captures raw user text (bounded 32 KiB, 64-session LRU). The in-process call passes `runtime: 'pi'` and races a timer of the advisor budget plus 300 ms, delivering the fallback directives if the call hangs | Model-visible message. Delivery-state machine suppresses same-session re-delivery |\n | **OpenCode** | `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` | Plugin, loaded by OpenCode's flat glob over `.opencode/plugins/`. `experimental.chat.system.transform` per prompt, `event` for session lifecycle, `tool` for status | Shells out to the advisor CLI (bounded timeout, stdout cap 256 KiB, grace termination), TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup, transform-dedup integration | Brief appended to `output.system`. `spec_kit_skill_advisor_status` tool returns a key=value report (no raw path leakage) |\n+| **DSH** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/dsh/prompt-advisor.mjs`, imported by `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/prompt-advisor.mjs` | `agent/pre-step` through the `repo-guards` plugin, on unless the `promptAdvisor` row flag is `false` | Proxies the latest operator prompt through the compiled Claude advisor chain under the runtime label `dsh`. A fan-out leaf, marked by the lineage patch, gets no brief | Brief added to the step |\n \n OpenCode has no source-hook adapter in `system-spec-kit`. Its prompt-time integration is the committed plugin, which shells out to the advisor CLI and inherits the shared render contract. The hooks-tree index at `.skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/` mirrors the per-runtime shims and the OpenCode plugin via relative symlinks. This README is the symlink target.\n \n+### Starting a surfaced command\n+\n+The document behind a surfaced command is `.skilled/commands/<group>/<name>.md`. The model reads that document in full and follows it, on every runtime. It never tells the user to type a slash command, and it asks the user to type one only when it cannot read the file.\n+\n+| Runtime | Model can start a command itself | Basis |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| Claude Code | Yes, through the Skill tool, as a shortcut for the names it lists: 33 of the 45 names, including one `create` entry and none of the other twelve `create:*` names | The Skill tool lists names, not files. `create/skill.md` collides with the `SKILL.md` convention on a case-insensitive disk, so the folder reads as one skill |\n+| Codex | No | The mirror is a pointer stub that tells the model to read the command file. There is no `.codex/skills` |\n+| Cursor | No model path found, and the brief is dormant | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Cursor docs were not fetched |\n+| Devin | No command surface at all | Skills are exposed, commands are not |\n+| Pi | No model path found | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Pi docs were not fetched, so this is unverified beyond the repository |\n+| OpenCode | No documented model path. A parent process can start one with `opencode run --command` | Commands are user-triggered templates, while skills are loaded by the model through a skill tool |\n+| DSH | No | dsh has no agent-file loader and no command loader |\n+\n ---\n \n ## 4. DIRECTORY TREE\n@@ -71,6 +121,9 @@ system-skill-advisor/hooks/\n |   +-- README.md\n |   +-- directive-lifecycle-boundary.ts\n |   `-- user-prompt-submit.ts         # real advisor handler (Claude shape)\n++-- dsh/\n+|   +-- README.md\n+|   `-- prompt-advisor.mjs            # real DSH adapter (.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/ imports it)\n +-- pi/\n |   +-- README.md\n |   `-- prompt-advisor.ts             # real Pi extension (.pi/extensions/ re-exports it)\n@@ -120,7 +173,7 @@ The advisor is enabled by default. Truthy disable values are `1`, `true`, `yes`,\n | `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` | Legacy alias for the OpenCode plugin. |\n | `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED=1` | Master switch that disables this concern along with every other repo hook. |\n \n-Additional controls: `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_FORCE_LOCAL=1` (force local Python fallback where supported) and `--force-native` / `--force-local` / `--threshold` / `--stdin` (Python shim flags, default threshold `0.8`). `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 3000) is owned by the `system-skill-advisor` hub: its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts`, `skill-advisor-brief.ts`, the OpenCode bridge and `skill_advisor.py`. On timeout the OpenCode bridge serves prompt-safe stale context with a timeout marker. OpenCode tuning: `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CACHE_TTL_MS` (default 5 min), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_TOKENS` (default 80), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_BRIEF_CHARS` (default 2 KiB), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_PROMPT_BYTES` (default 64 KiB, measured on the whole stdin request after JSON escaping), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_COMPILED_ROUTE_BOUNDING` (compiled-route summary bounding).\n+Additional controls: `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_FORCE_LOCAL=1` (force local Python fallback where supported) and `--force-native` / `--force-local` / `--threshold` / `--stdin` (Python shim flags, default threshold `0.8`). `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 3000) is owned by the `system-skill-advisor` hub: its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts`, `skill-advisor-brief.ts`, the OpenCode bridge and `skill_advisor.py`. On timeout the OpenCode bridge serves prompt-safe stale context with a timeout marker. OpenCode tuning: `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CACHE_TTL_MS` (default 5 min), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_TOKENS` (default 80), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_BRIEF_CHARS` (default 2 KiB), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_PROMPT_BYTES` (default 64 KiB, measured on the whole stdin request after JSON escaping), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_COMPILED_ROUTE_BOUNDING` (kept for existing configs: the transform adds no compiled-route line, so it changes only the `bounded_compiled_route_summary` status field).\n \n Set a flag inline for one command, export it for a session, or persist it in `.skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env` (copied from `hook-flags.env.example`, gitignored). The environment always wins over the file, so a persisted default can be overridden for a single session.\n \n@@ -135,7 +188,7 @@ Set a flag inline for one command, export it for a session, or persist it in `.s\n | Freshness | Freshness states `live`, `stale`, `absent`, `unavailable`. Status values `ok`, `skipped`, `degraded`, `fail_open`. Default confidence/uncertainty `0.8 / 0.35` unless overridden. |\n | Privacy | Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution. The OpenCode status tool sanitizes paths (`[configured-node]`, `[skill-advisor-bridge]`). |\n | Dedup | Directive-lifecycle dedup suppresses re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence. OpenCode adds transform-dedup across co-resident transforms. The plugin decides same-message transform dedup on the full advisor block before lifecycle reduction, so a repeated transform for the same message is suppressed. |\n-| Diagnostics | Each hook record carries `runtime` (the Codex, Cursor and Devin adapters set `SPECKIT_RUNTIME` and Pi passes `pi`) plus `emittedBytes` and `directivesSuppressed`. Records are written only with `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` set, and the subprocess hook waits up to 100 ms for pending writes before exit. |\n+| Diagnostics | Each hook record carries `runtime` (the Codex, Cursor and Devin adapters set `SPECKIT_RUNTIME` and Pi passes `pi`) plus `emittedBytes` and `directivesSuppressed`. Every prompt writes a health record to the diagnostics sink with no prompt, skill label or error text, and an answer from the CLI's local scorer carries `degraded: true`. `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` adds the skill label and the error text. The subprocess hook waits up to 100 ms for pending writes before exit. |\n | Bounded | Shims bound stdin (1 MiB), child stdio (1 MiB), child timeout (2.5s), and `SIGKILL` on timeout. OpenCode bounds bridge stdout (256 KiB) and uses grace termination. Pi bounds captured user text (32 KiB, 64-session LRU). |\n | Output | Native adapters emit one `additionalContext` JSON on stdout. OpenCode appends to `output.system`. Pi sends a model-visible message. No adapter writes to the TUI prompt line. |\n | Imports | Shims import Node builtins only and spawn the advisor by absolute path. The advisor core imports its `runtime/lib/*` siblings and the shared `hook-flags` resolver. Nothing outside the repo. |\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\nindex 883ac30d82..22b508c123 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\n@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@ trigger_phrases:\n   - \"native recommend\"\n   - \"skill recommendation tool\"\n   - \"compiledRoute enrichment\"\n-version: 0.9.1.0\n+version: 0.9.4.0\n ---\n \n # advisor_recommend Command\n@@ -28,9 +28,23 @@ The caller-supplied `workspaceRoot` is bounded by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.\n \n ### Compiled-Routing Enrichment (`compiledRoute`)\n \n-Each recommendation carries an optional `compiledRoute` field (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:268`, `z.record(z.string(), z.unknown()).optional()`). `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes()` attaches it only when at least one recommended `skillId` is both in the fixed 7-hub `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` set and currently permitted by the tri-state `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag (`compiledRoutingEnabledForHub()`, from `../lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts`). Otherwise the schema re-parse is skipped entirely and the output returns unchanged. When enrichment runs, `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub <skillId> --prompt-stdin`, writes the prompt to its stdin and attaches the parsed result. The one exception is a result whose `servingAuthority` is `\"legacy\"`: the field then stays absent, because a legacy sentinel is never surfaced as if it were a compiled decision. A subprocess failure is handled the same way: silently no `compiledRoute` field, with an optional debug-gated stderr breadcrumb via `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING_DEBUG`, so a probe failure degrades to exactly what an ineligible or flag-off recommendation already looks like. In short, `compiledRoute` is attached only for an eligible, flag-permitted hub that is genuinely serving compiled. It is intentionally absent for every other combination: flag off, hub not in `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS`, legacy sentinel or probe failure. See [`feature-flag-governance.md`](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) for the flag's phased-default and serving-status contract, and each eligible hub's own `compiled-routing-and-legacy-fallback.md` catalog leaf for the hub-side directive this enrichment mirrors. The front door runs from the request's `workspaceRoot`, and a caller may name another checkout there, such as a worktree under the repository root. So the handler first checks that the front door it would run reads `--prompt-stdin`. An older front door gets no call and the recommendation carries no `compiledRoute`, because it would route an empty prompt.\n+Each recommendation carries an optional `compiledRoute` field (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:308`, `z.record(z.string(), z.unknown()).optional()`). `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes()` attaches it only when at least one recommended `skillId` is both in the fixed 7-hub `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` set and currently permitted by the tri-state `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag (`compiledRoutingEnabledForHub()`, from `../lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts`). Otherwise the schema re-parse is skipped entirely and the output returns unchanged. When enrichment runs, `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub <skillId> --prompt-stdin`, writes the prompt to its stdin and attaches the parsed result. The one exception is a result whose `servingAuthority` is `\"legacy\"`: the field then stays absent, because a legacy sentinel is never surfaced as if it were a compiled decision. A subprocess failure is handled the same way: silently no `compiledRoute` field, with an optional debug-gated stderr breadcrumb via `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING_DEBUG`, so a probe failure degrades to exactly what an ineligible or flag-off recommendation already looks like. In short, `compiledRoute` is attached only for an eligible, flag-permitted hub that is genuinely serving compiled. It is intentionally absent for every other combination: flag off, hub not in `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS`, legacy sentinel or probe failure. See [`feature-flag-governance.md`](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) for the flag's phased-default and serving-status contract, and each eligible hub's own `compiled-routing-and-legacy-fallback.md` catalog leaf for the hub-side directive this enrichment mirrors. The front door runs from the request's `workspaceRoot`, and a caller may name another checkout there, such as a worktree under the repository root. So the handler first checks that the front door it would run reads `--prompt-stdin`. An older front door gets no call and the recommendation carries no `compiledRoute`, because it would route an empty prompt.\n \n-The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:500-510`, `570-576`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute`. The prompt hooks always pass `false` because they never read the field (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:230`). The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:227`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:40`).\n+When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:310`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must type the same command or hold a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command. A prompt that matches `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` gets no command from any hub, because a reader who rules out a change is never handed work that writes.\n+\n+The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:563-565`, `632-634`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:287`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n+\n+The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute` or `command`. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:290`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n+\n+A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\n+\n+### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n+\n+Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n+\n+`lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, mutating?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `mutating` is the class the renderer's child downgrade reads: `/doctor:env list` carries `read-only` and stays fully visible to a dispatched child, while `start`, `ask` and a writing `run` are marked as parent-named. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy`, the table keyed by runnable string that covers every tracked command, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n+\n+The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n \n ---\n \n@@ -43,6 +57,7 @@ The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that de\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` | Handler | Source reference |\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` | Schema | Source reference |\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tools/` | Command surface | Source reference |\n+| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Shared | `attachCommand()`, the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table and the `DEPTH_WORDING` gate that name the slash command for a single routed mode. |\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts` | Shared | `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` eligibility set, `DEFAULT_ON_HUBS` cohort, and the tri-state flag parser the enrichment gate reads. |\n | `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs` | Script | Promoted CLI front door `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to. |\n \n@@ -51,7 +66,7 @@ The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that de\n | File | Type | Role |\n |---|---|---|\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n-| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. |\n+| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. `compiledRouteTopOnly: true` looks up the first recommendation alone. A command that ranks through its own row is not offered again by its hub. |\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-privacy.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/compiled-routing-consumption.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Bridge child-env allowlist forwarding, `buildNativeBrief` decision threading, and `=0` cache-invalidation coverage for the `compiledRoute` enrichment. |\n | `.skilled/bin/compiled-routing-flag-propagation.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Tri-state flag propagation coverage. |\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md\nindex 20f4fb025f..467e47a634 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md\n@@ -6,7 +6,7 @@ trigger_phrases:\n   - \"claude user-prompt-submit\"\n   - \"hookSpecificOutput claude\"\n   - \"claude advisor hook\"\n-version: 0.11.3.0\n+version: 0.11.5.0\n ---\n \n # Claude Code user-prompt-submit Hook\n@@ -23,7 +23,7 @@ Surface skill recommendations in Claude Code sessions at prompt time, without bl\n \n `hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` reads the prompt from stdin, calls the native advisor and returns a JSON envelope with `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. The hook honors `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` and fails open on any daemon-level failure. As a default-off, fail-open shadow it also records post-emission advisor-policy delivery (`observeEmittedAdvisorPolicy`), an observed receipt with `lifecycleEpoch >= 1`, as the final step before returning, leaving the emitted envelope byte-identical. Freshness vocabulary is `live / stale / absent / unavailable`. Status vocabulary is `ok / skipped / degraded / fail_open`.\n \n-Before any CLI spawn the hook runs the casual-prompt gate. `/help`, short acknowledgements and prompts below the length threshold return `skipped` without spawning the CLI. The CLI request sends `includeCompiledRoute: false`, so no compiled-route child process starts.\n+Before any CLI spawn the hook runs the casual-prompt gate. `/help`, short acknowledgements and prompts below the length threshold return `skipped` without spawning the CLI. The CLI request sends `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. It also sends `compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, so only the top pick gets a route lookup and only the top pick can carry a command. The hook keeps the `command` field on each recommendation and reads nothing from the route itself.\n \n ### Fallback And Repeats\n \n@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@ Directive-lifecycle dedup treats that fallback like a brief. In a known, confirm\n \n Hook diagnostics are prompt-safe rollups handled by `lib/metrics.ts`. The `spec_kit.<group>.<metric_name>` instrumentation namespace emits only when `SPECKIT_METRICS_ENABLED=true`. Default off is no emission and is byte-identical to the pre-instrumentation surface. Alert thresholds for the rolling cache-hit p95 are read from the environment with conservative defaults: `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_HOOK_CACHE_HIT_P95_WARN_MS` (default 75) and `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_HOOK_CACHE_HIT_P95_PAGE_MS` (default 150). Both flags are forwarded to the daemon child through the launcher `CHILD_ENV_ALLOWLIST` so the same thresholds reach every runtime.\n \n-Each record carries `runtime`, taken from `SPECKIT_RUNTIME` when it names a known runtime and `claude` otherwise. Records carry optional `emittedBytes` and `directivesSuppressed`. The hook writes records only when `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` is set. Raw prompts never appear in diagnostics. Before exiting, the hook waits up to 100 ms for pending diagnostic writes.\n+Each record carries `runtime`, taken from `SPECKIT_RUNTIME` when it names a known runtime and `claude` otherwise. Records carry optional `emittedBytes` and `directivesSuppressed`. The hook writes a health record to the diagnostics sink on every prompt and adds the skill label and the error text there only when `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` is set. A record whose answer came from the CLI's local scorer carries `degraded: true`. Raw prompts never appear in diagnostics. Before exiting, the hook waits up to 100 ms for pending diagnostic writes.\n \n ---\n \ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\nindex bddf8cc99a..a6b24e56bb 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\n@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@ trigger_phrases:\n   - \"advisor hook smoke tests\"\n importance_tier: important\n contextType: implementation\n-version: 3.6.5.0\n+version: 3.6.9.0\n ---\n \n # Skill Advisor Hook Reference\n@@ -35,12 +35,47 @@ On each user-prompt event the adapter:\n 1. **Kill-switch.** Checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` (master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED` or the `skill-advisor` family). Disabled → empty/skipped prompt-safe output.\n 2. **Read the prompt + workspace.** Resolves the workspace root (install-anchored walk, not CWD-relative, so the hook stays correct off-root), reads the prompt text (bounded), and resolves the session id.\n 3. **Prompt gate.** `skippedAdvisorResultFor` uses `shouldFireAdvisor` to decline skip commands, short acknowledgements and prompts below its length threshold. A declined prompt returns a skipped result and does not spawn the CLI.\n-4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: false`, so the daemon skips compiled-route enrichment and starts no `compiled-route.cjs` child. A daemon still running an older build rejects that option with JSON-RPC `-32602` and the CLI retries once without it.\n-5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n+4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. It also carries `compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, because each route lookup is a subprocess inside the hook's budget and the brief leads with the top pick. The hook keeps the `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. A daemon still running an older build rejects these options with JSON-RPC `-32602`, and the CLI retries once without them.\n+5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. With `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`, a surfaced `start` or `ask` command, or a `run` command that is not read-only, renders as `(parent-named only)`. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n 6. **Directive-lifecycle dedup.** `decideDirectiveLifecycleDelivery` suppresses a re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence, so a repeated prompt does not re-inject an identical brief. A repeat in a known session keeps the first line, a route or a fallback status, and drops the directives block.\n 7. **Emit.** Native adapters emit a `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON envelope. OpenCode appends to `output.system` via `experimental.chat.system.transform`. Pi sends a model-visible message.\n \n-The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the recommended skill(s) with their confidence. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n+The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n+\n+### Brief head form\n+\n+A recommendation that resolves to a command renders one label, shared by the `use` and `ambiguous` heads:\n+\n+```text\n+<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\n+```\n+\n+The recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A recommendation with no command row keeps its skill label rather than a command label.\n+\n+The `use` head reads `Advisor: <freshness>; use <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` For example:\n+\n+```text\n+Advisor: live; use system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start) 0.89/0.12 pass.\n+```\n+\n+The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\n+\n+A hub whose compiled route defers or asks to clarify, and whose modes have commands, renders as `<hub> (needs a mode)` with no command, for example:\n+\n+```text\n+Advisor: live; use system-deep-loop (needs a mode) 0.82/0.20 pass.\n+```\n+\n+The recommendation carries `needsMode: true`. A command on the same recommendation wins and the label is dropped.\n+\n+### Degraded contract\n+\n+When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\n+\n+- A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n+- Every other row collapses to its owner skill: `skillId` is `ownerSkillId` and `kind` is `skill`. It is dropped when that skill is already offered, so the degraded brief never repeats a skill.\n+\n+Rows below the threshold are discarded before either rule applies. The degraded brief therefore keeps the same head form and names a skill that exists rather than a raw row id.\n \n ---\n \n@@ -56,9 +91,24 @@ Every native runtime and Pi run the same hook handler, which gates the prompt, c\n | **Devin** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.devin/hooks.v1.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 10s | Same shim dispatch. `cd \"${DEVIN_PROJECT_DIR:-$PWD}\"`. Fallback `printf` envelope | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n | **Pi** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` (real file, re-exported by its one-line `.pi/extensions/` entry) | `user_input` / prompt event via `.pi/extensions/` | Imports the advisor module **in-process** (not a subprocess). Pi awaits input handlers before agent processing, so the old blocking-spawn bridge stalled every send. In-process calls remove the stall and let the module-level prompt cache work. Captures raw user text (bounded 32 KiB, 64-session LRU). The in-process call passes `runtime: 'pi'` and races a timer of the advisor budget plus 300 ms, delivering the fallback directives if the call hangs | Model-visible message. Delivery-state machine suppresses same-session re-delivery |\n | **OpenCode** | `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` | Plugin, loaded by OpenCode's flat glob over `.opencode/plugins/`. `experimental.chat.system.transform` per prompt, `event` for session lifecycle, `tool` for status | Shells out to the advisor CLI (bounded timeout, stdout cap 256 KiB, grace termination), TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup, transform-dedup integration | Brief appended to `output.system`. `spec_kit_skill_advisor_status` tool returns a key=value report (no raw path leakage) |\n+| **DSH** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/dsh/prompt-advisor.mjs`, imported by `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/prompt-advisor.mjs` | `agent/pre-step` through the `repo-guards` plugin, on unless the `promptAdvisor` row flag is `false` | Proxies the latest operator prompt through the compiled Claude advisor chain under the runtime label `dsh`. A fan-out leaf, marked by the lineage patch, gets no brief | Brief added to the step |\n \n OpenCode has no source-hook adapter in `system-spec-kit`. Its prompt-time integration is the committed plugin, which shells out to the advisor CLI and inherits the shared render contract. The hooks-tree index at `.skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/` mirrors the per-runtime shims and the OpenCode plugin via relative symlinks. This README is the symlink target.\n \n+### Starting a surfaced command\n+\n+The document behind a surfaced command is `.skilled/commands/<group>/<name>.md`. The model reads that document in full and follows it, on every runtime. It never tells the user to type a slash command, and it asks the user to type one only when it cannot read the file.\n+\n+| Runtime | Model can start a command itself | Basis |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| Claude Code | Yes, through the Skill tool, as a shortcut for the names it lists: 33 of the 45 names, including one `create` entry and none of the other twelve `create:*` names | The Skill tool lists names, not files. `create/skill.md` collides with the `SKILL.md` convention on a case-insensitive disk, so the folder reads as one skill |\n+| Codex | No | The mirror is a pointer stub that tells the model to read the command file. There is no `.codex/skills` |\n+| Cursor | No model path found, and the brief is dormant | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Cursor docs were not fetched |\n+| Devin | No command surface at all | Skills are exposed, commands are not |\n+| Pi | No model path found | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Pi docs were not fetched, so this is unverified beyond the repository |\n+| OpenCode | No documented model path. A parent process can start one with `opencode run --command` | Commands are user-triggered templates, while skills are loaded by the model through a skill tool |\n+| DSH | No | dsh has no agent-file loader and no command loader |\n+\n ---\n \n ## 4. DIRECTORY TREE\n@@ -71,6 +121,9 @@ system-skill-advisor/hooks/\n |   +-- README.md\n |   +-- directive-lifecycle-boundary.ts\n |   `-- user-prompt-submit.ts         # real advisor handler (Claude shape)\n++-- dsh/\n+|   +-- README.md\n+|   `-- prompt-advisor.mjs            # real DSH adapter (.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/ imports it)\n +-- pi/\n |   +-- README.md\n |   `-- prompt-advisor.ts             # real Pi extension (.pi/extensions/ re-exports it)\n@@ -120,7 +173,7 @@ The advisor is enabled by default. Truthy disable values are `1`, `true`, `yes`,\n | `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` | Legacy alias for the OpenCode plugin. |\n | `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED=1` | Master switch that disables this concern along with every other repo hook. |\n \n-Additional controls: `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_FORCE_LOCAL=1` (force local Python fallback where supported) and `--force-native` / `--force-local` / `--threshold` / `--stdin` (Python shim flags, default threshold `0.8`). `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 3000) is owned by the `system-skill-advisor` hub: its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts`, `skill-advisor-brief.ts`, the OpenCode bridge and `skill_advisor.py`. On timeout the OpenCode bridge serves prompt-safe stale context with a timeout marker. OpenCode tuning: `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CACHE_TTL_MS` (default 5 min), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_TOKENS` (default 80), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_BRIEF_CHARS` (default 2 KiB), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_PROMPT_BYTES` (default 64 KiB, measured on the whole stdin request after JSON escaping), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_COMPILED_ROUTE_BOUNDING` (compiled-route summary bounding).\n+Additional controls: `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_FORCE_LOCAL=1` (force local Python fallback where supported) and `--force-native` / `--force-local` / `--threshold` / `--stdin` (Python shim flags, default threshold `0.8`). `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 3000) is owned by the `system-skill-advisor` hub: its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts`, `skill-advisor-brief.ts`, the OpenCode bridge and `skill_advisor.py`. On timeout the OpenCode bridge serves prompt-safe stale context with a timeout marker. OpenCode tuning: `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CACHE_TTL_MS` (default 5 min), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_TOKENS` (default 80), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_BRIEF_CHARS` (default 2 KiB), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_PROMPT_BYTES` (default 64 KiB, measured on the whole stdin request after JSON escaping), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_COMPILED_ROUTE_BOUNDING` (kept for existing configs: the transform adds no compiled-route line, so it changes only the `bounded_compiled_route_summary` status field).\n \n Set a flag inline for one command, export it for a session, or persist it in `.skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env` (copied from `hook-flags.env.example`, gitignored). The environment always wins over the file, so a persisted default can be overridden for a single session.\n \n@@ -135,7 +188,7 @@ Set a flag inline for one command, export it for a session, or persist it in `.s\n | Freshness | Freshness states `live`, `stale`, `absent`, `unavailable`. Status values `ok`, `skipped`, `degraded`, `fail_open`. Default confidence/uncertainty `0.8 / 0.35` unless overridden. |\n | Privacy | Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution. The OpenCode status tool sanitizes paths (`[configured-node]`, `[skill-advisor-bridge]`). |\n | Dedup | Directive-lifecycle dedup suppresses re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence. OpenCode adds transform-dedup across co-resident transforms. The plugin decides same-message transform dedup on the full advisor block before lifecycle reduction, so a repeated transform for the same message is suppressed. |\n-| Diagnostics | Each hook record carries `runtime` (the Codex, Cursor and Devin adapters set `SPECKIT_RUNTIME` and Pi passes `pi`) plus `emittedBytes` and `directivesSuppressed`. Records are written only with `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` set, and the subprocess hook waits up to 100 ms for pending writes before exit. |\n+| Diagnostics | Each hook record carries `runtime` (the Codex, Cursor and Devin adapters set `SPECKIT_RUNTIME` and Pi passes `pi`) plus `emittedBytes` and `directivesSuppressed`. Every prompt writes a health record to the diagnostics sink with no prompt, skill label or error text, and an answer from the CLI's local scorer carries `degraded: true`. `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` adds the skill label and the error text. The subprocess hook waits up to 100 ms for pending writes before exit. |\n | Bounded | Shims bound stdin (1 MiB), child stdio (1 MiB), child timeout (2.5s), and `SIGKILL` on timeout. OpenCode bounds bridge stdout (256 KiB) and uses grace termination. Pi bounds captured user text (32 KiB, 64-session LRU). |\n | Output | Native adapters emit one `additionalContext` JSON on stdout. OpenCode appends to `output.system`. Pi sends a model-visible message. No adapter writes to the TUI prompt line. |\n | Imports | Shims import Node builtins only and spawn the advisor by absolute path. The advisor core imports its `runtime/lib/*` siblings and the shared `hook-flags` resolver. Nothing outside the repo. |\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && git diff 35c3188681..HEAD -- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794571183874,"end":1794571184267}}}}
